  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1216 PM MDT Tue Oct 27 2026\n\nCurrently there is weak zonal flow aloft over southern Idaho. \nUpstream, there is a weak upper level low over southwest Oregon. \nModels show this weak upper level low moving through zonal flow \ntonight into Wednesday morning over our area. Very little \nprecipitation is expected with this system. Models show one band of \nprecipitation stretching from Driggs to Sun Valley with a .01 to .05 \ninch of QPF. Models also show up to around 0.10 of QPF in the Island \nPark Region, the Bear Lake Region, and the northern Central \nmountains. Winds will remain breezy mainly for ridge tops and in the \nupper Snake River Plain today with gusts around 20 to 30 mph.\nPatchy fog near water sources is possible tonight into\nWednesday.\n\nWednesday will be nearly a repeat of today, though drier as we \nbriefly return to southwesterly flow aloft ahead of the next system \nas temperatures warm into the upper 40s to mid 50s on Thursday \nafternoon. Again, patchy fog is possible near water sources\nWednesday night into Thursday. \n\nAn area of low pressure off of the coast of the PNW brings moisture \ninto Eastern Idaho for the weekend, but will begin to bring \nprecipitation back to us as early as overnight Thursday into Friday. \nMoisture moving into the area Thursday night has back off some with \nmore moisture coming in Friday night into Saturday. Currently, \nSaturday afternoon/early evening (Halloween) moisture looks to be \naround 0.02 to 0.10 inch of rain across the area. However, some\nmodels hold off moisture until late Saturday into Sunday \nmorning when deep troughing over much of the West pulls ample \nPacific moisture into Idaho. This system will keep our highs in \nthe 40s and 50s on Saturday, and 40s for Sunday and Monday. \n\nWe will continue to see how precipitation totals change over the \nnext several model runs, so stay tuned for those amounts as we get a \nlittle closer to it.",
